"There were a total of 14 kitchen staff members and two climbing guides from different companies who were missing. The two climbing guides have been found, but sadly, both were found deceased," NNMGA wrote in a statement posted on Facebook.

The association's president, Tul Singh Gurung, told dpa that more search and rescue workers were heading to the area for a search and rescue operation.

Inspector Binod Bohara, a police officer in the affected Manang district, said his office has received a list of at least 22 people who were at the base camp of Himlung Himal, a 7,126-metre peak near the border with Tibet, when the avalanche hit the area on Sunday morning.

